8. IP Address Conflict Check
We discussed how to check used IP addresses in the SML subnet.
PowerShell ping scan:
```powershell
1..254 | ForEach-Object { Test-Connection -ComputerName "172.16.50.$_" -Count 1 -Quiet | Where-Object { $_ } | ForEach-Object { "172.16.50.$_ is responding" } }
```
This command pings every address from `172.16.50.1` to `172.16.50.254`.
It helps identify IP addresses that respond to ICMP. It is useful for a quick scan, but it is not perfect because some machines may block ping.
ARP check:
```powershell
arp -a
```
This command shows the local ARP table.
It helps confirm which IP addresses your machine recently resolved to MAC addresses.
Specific `.60` check:
```powershell
Test-Connection 172.16.50.60 -Count 4
```
This sends four pings to `172.16.50.60`.
If it replies, the address is already in use or at least active. If it times out, it is probably free, but DHCP leases and documentation should still be checked.
```powershell
arp -a | findstr 172.16.50.60
```
This searches the ARP table for `172.16.50.60`.
If it returns a MAC address, something may already be using that IP.
